<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durand Line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Durand Line is the border of about 2,640 kilometres between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, fixed in an 1893 agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> and Sir Mortimer Durand, foreign secretary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. It divided Pashtun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> tribal lands between the two states, and successive Afghan governments have refused to recognise it as an international boundary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durand-line.jpg" alt="Durand Line" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Weaveravel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afghanistan-Pakistan_border.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
